[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What stack should this be built on? → Vite + TypeScript, no framework (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Design, in sections. I'll pause after each.

  ---

  [1mSection 1 — Scope and module architecture[0m

  [1mScope.[0m Because the repo is empty, this spec necessarily covers three things, in dependency order: a task model with
  persistence, due dates on tasks, and the notification system on top. I want to name that explicitly so the spec isn't
  read as "notifications only" — roughly half the work is the substrate.

  [1mArchitecture.[0m Seven modules, organized so the time logic is isolated from everything that makes it hard to test:

  - [38;5;153mmodel/task.ts[39m — the [38;5;153mTask[39m type and pure helpers (create, complete, set due date). No I/O.
  - [38;5;153mstore/tasks.ts[39m — load/save to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, with a schema version field so a future format change has somewhere to
    hook.
  - [38;5;153mnotifications/engine.ts[39m — [1mpure[0m. Signature is roughly [38;5;153m(tasks, now, leadTime, alreadyFired) => NotificationEvent[][39m. No
    timers, no storage, no DOM. This is where "due soon", "now due", and "overdue" are decided.
  - [38;5;153mnotifications/scheduler.ts[39m — the only module that owns a timer and reads the real clock. Wakes up, calls the engine,
    hands results to the store. Impure by design and deliberately thin.
  - [38;5;153mnotifications/store.ts[39m — notification records with read/unread state, persisted.
  - [38;5;153mui/[39m — task list, notification panel with unread badge, and a thin adapter to the browser Notification API.
  - [38;5;153mmain.ts[39m — wiring only.

  The load-bearing boundary is engine vs. scheduler. Every interesting rule lives in a pure function that takes [38;5;153mnow[39m as
  an argument, so the whole test suite runs on a fake clock with no waiting. The scheduler, which is the part that's
  genuinely awkward to test, ends up with almost no logic in it.

  Does that split look right, and are you comfortable with the spec covering the task substrate as well as
  notifications?

